# 🚢 Titanic Survival Predictor — ML-Powered Web App

An interactive machine learning web application that predicts passenger survival on the Titanic based on passenger details. Built with Python and Streamlit, this project demonstrates how a trained machine learning model can be integrated into a user-friendly interface and deployed as a public web application.

## 🎯 Project Objective

The objective of this project is to build and deploy a usable web interface for a trained machine learning model, demonstrating the practical application of model serving and deployment.

This project is part of the **Evolvix AI/ML Internship Program — Week 4, Task 2: Applied AI & Model Deployment**.

## ✨ Features

- Interactive user interface built with Streamlit.
- Passenger information input form.
- Survival prediction using a trained Logistic Regression model.
- Prediction results displayed in a simple, understandable format.
- Model explanation and limitations for users.
- Public deployment for easy access.

## 🛠️ Technologies Used

- **Python** — application development
- **Streamlit** — web interface
- **Pandas** — data handling
- **Scikit-learn** — machine learning
- **Joblib** — model loading

## 🧠 Machine Learning Model

The application uses a previously trained Logistic Regression model saved as a `.pkl` file.

The model uses passenger-related features to predict one of two classes:

- **Survived**
- **Did Not Survive**

The application must provide the same input features and preprocessing expected by the original trained model.

